// upstream construct: lines 8-22 — `S(_s)` in match / let / fn-arg where `impl Drop for S`; //~ ERROR cannot move out of type `S`, which implements the `Drop` trait
// Modifications: none — NOT PORTED AS-IS AND NOT PORTABLE AS-IS. The construct
//   cannot be made illegal here: Logos deliberately ADMITS a move out of a field
//   of a value with a user `Drop`, and defines what its destructor then does —
//   `intrinsic.drop.skip-moved-out-paths` (docs/spec/expressions.md), "Drop of a
//   value suppresses sub-values that were moved out: a dotted path whose segment
//   exactly matches a child skips that child's drop entirely". E0509 exists in
//   Rust precisely because Rust has no such rule.
// ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-09-07 (same day, later round). The paragraph that stood here
//   claimed the clause was "RE-VERIFIED BY RUNNING IT ... prints INNER_DROPS=1
//   S_DROPS=1 ... No leak, no double free". THAT RUN USED THE DOTTED-PATH DOOR
//   (`let g = s.f;`). THIS PORT USES THE STRUCT-PATTERN DOOR (`let S { f: inner }
//   = s;`), and at that door the clause is NOT implemented: this program's own
//   body returns `DROPS * 10 + x`, and it exits 1 on build f812a8b998ecde2b —
//   DROPS = 0, `impl Drop for S` NEVER RAN. A struct-pattern destructure of a
//   value with a user `Drop` also leaks any field the pattern discards, while the
//   dotted-path and tuple-struct-pattern doors are both correct (111 vs 100 on a
//   per-type destructor-counter sweep of seven doors).
//   THE ROW BELOW STILL STANDS AND STILL BELONGS HERE: what is blessed is the
//   ADMISSION (Logos deliberately does not raise E0509), and that is unchanged.
//   What was wrong was the EVIDENCE, which was taken at a door this program does
//   not use. The run-time defect is now its own tier-1 row —
//   `letstruct_destructure_skips_user_drop` in tests/logos/soundness_queue.ledger
//   — so the divergence is blessed and the misbehaviour is counted, separately.
// The file stays on the admit shelf and keeps its row in
// tests/logos/bc_admits_blocked.ledger; it is not a hole and never was.
package borrowck_move_out_of_tuple_struct_with_dtor_t13;
struct Inner { a: i64 }
struct S { f: Inner }
impl Drop for S { fn drop(self: &mut S) {} }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let s = S { f: Inner { a: 1i64 } };
    let S { f: inner } = s;
    return inner.a as i32;
}
